<Layout title="About | Minh-Ha Nguyen" description="About Minh-Ha Nguyen — PhD candidate in Epidemiology at Vanderbilt, working on medication safety and large-language-model evaluation">
  <div class="about-page">
    <ParticleHero name="Minh-Ha Nguyen" subtitle="About" />

    <div class="about-content">
      <section class="intro">
        <p class="lead">
          I'm a PhD candidate in Epidemiology at Vanderbilt, working in two worlds that keep
          turning out to be the same one: medication safety, and the evaluation of large
          language models. Right now NASA funds me to ask whether chronic low-dose radiation
          raises the risk of Parkinson's disease, in the sailors who lived closest to that
          question.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="body">
        <p>
          Those two worlds sound unrelated, but to me they are one problem in different clothes:
          how do you draw a conclusion you can actually trust from messy, observational data?
          With a medication, the data are millions of patient records, and the question is
          whether a drug is truly harmful or whether the signal is an artifact of how the study
          was built. With a language model, the data are its answers to test cases, and the
          question is whether a benchmark score means what people say it means. Either way, the
          hard part is knowing whether to believe the number.
        </p>
        <p>
          My doctoral work is that problem at its hardest. I study Navy submariners, men who
          spent years under chronic low-dose radiation in a sealed hull, about the closest thing
          on Earth to a long mission in space. Does that exposure raise the risk of Parkinson's?
          The catch is that everything accumulates together: the men who served longest absorbed
          the most dose, and were the healthiest to begin with, a self-selection that can make a
          real hazard look protective. The craft is in designing an analysis where the radiation
          signal survives it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The other half of my work brings the same craft to AI, with one liberating difference:
          here I can run the experiment the radiation study never allows. I build systems that
          reason from a patient's symptoms toward a rare-disease diagnosis, and I evaluate them
          the way an epidemiologist would: a model is a population you sample from, a prompt is
          a treatment you assign, and a benchmark is an experiment that can be designed well or
          badly. The latest of these systems is
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.16149" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">liteOdyssey</a>,
          which learns a diagnostic policy from clinician corrections. On 1,243 benchmark cases
          spanning 722 rare diseases, it ranked the correct disease first in 59.3% of cases,
          against 26.5% without the policy, and the gains held in 515 real patients from the
          Undiagnosed Diseases Network.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before building, I mapped the field. My
          <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41746-026-03109-z" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">systematic
          review and meta-analysis in npj Digital Medicine</a>
          pooled every evaluation of LLMs for rare-disease diagnosis: across 15 studies and
          nearly 40,000 cases, the correct diagnosis comes first only 43% of the time, and
          systems augmented with retrieval, tools, or fine-tuning clearly beat standalone
          models. That paper is the map; liteOdyssey is one attempt at the territory.
        </p>
        <p>
          Underneath both halves, I think research should be legible to the people it's about:
          open access, methods you can check, and as much honesty about uncertainty as about
          findings.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="background">
        <h2>Background</h2>
        <p>
          I came to epidemiology through pharmacy. I hold a Master of Pharmacy (MPharm) and am
          pursuing doctoral studies in Epidemiology at Vanderbilt University, with training
          across clinical pharmacy, epidemiology, and biostatistics. Off the clock I read about
          deep learning, algorithm design, and reinforcement learning, partly for fun, mostly
          because that is where my field is heading.
        </p>
      </section>

